frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Marimba Eb6 over La Favre's D#6 tube, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; its loss Hill's printed whole-bar loss factor in place of the three-bar fit; its elastic ratios the rosewood Beaton and Scavone (2021) print from Bork et al. (1999), its undercut solved again on them; everything else as the rendered Eb6 card, except a 56 x 12 x 6 mesh with the cut re-solved on this mesh, its boundary-element surface split along each grid face's shorter diagonal, 9 windows, the window factor and plan caps raised by the two rows at the end, and the certificate row at the end.
name	marimba-eb6-hill-tubetuned-rosewood-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: D# row, length 8 15/16 in; width 1 3/4 in and thickness 0.87 in from the E row. Tube: the same page, La Favre's own padouk marimba, D#6 resonator: aluminium tubing, outer diameter 2 in, wall 1/16 in, air column not measured, total resonator length 4 11/16 in at the tube's centre, straight, 3/4 in below the bottom of the bar. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: D. Beaton and G. Scavone, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 149(6), 3758 (2021), Table II p.3760, rosewood "adopted from the literature (Bork et al., 1999)", a C3 marimba bar: E 23.0, 2.3 and 1.15 GPa, G 3.0, 1.0 and 3.0 GPa (xy, yz, xz) and nu 0.30, 0.60 and 0.45 (xy, yz, xz), x along the bar, y across it and z through it; only the eight ratios to E_x are read, second hand, since Bork et al. (1999) is unread. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. La Favre prints no air column for the D#6 tube ("not measured"), only its total length of 4 11/16 in, so this card sets the stop as a maker would, at 53.283 mm (2.10 in), solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it for the cut before these ratios; on this bar it leaves the tube's first resonance +0.333 c from this bar's own computed f1 of 1244.515 Hz, from this card's modes, and the law here reads 53.296 mm, at which this card was not rendered; the tube's cross-section and gap are as printed and no recording enters. NOT in the source either: the undercut (solved here in the engine on this card's ratios and 56 x 12 x 6 mesh, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 34.925 mm, middle 12.957 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament. Reach is not solved: it is the arch length the builder's padouk table prints at this note, 2 3/4 in on a 9 in bar, because the rosewood table prints none, and the builder says this register is tuned to the fundamental only, so no f2 was aimed at; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. Tube and bar come from two instruments: the bar from the unnamed commercial table, the tube from La Favre's own marimba, printed at A4 = 442 and 72 F, rendered here at A4 = 440 in the card's gas; no correction made. "Bottom of bar" is read as the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 9.1 mm from the mouth. The tube is a closed-open column of one odd mode, the only one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 47.6 mm across inside, is wider than this 44.4 mm bar. The loss is one printed whole-bar loss factor, eta 0.0045 at every mode, the mean as printed (the printed range, 0.0036 to 0.0068, is its limit), from H. Hill's unrefereed 2011 student report (NSF REU, University of Illinois Physics 406): the bars of a Yamaha YM-5100A, Honduras rosewood per the maker's spec for that model, recorded in a room with the resonators off, so eta holds the wood, the cord and the bar's own radiation, and this card's tube adds only its own losses. The stiffness and density stay the rosewood rows cited above, so the loss and the stiffness come from different rosewoods. It was tried after seeing that the pages decay too fast on the three-bar fit, with no recording number in it. The 9 windows and the 1e-7 row come from the cut before these ratios, on which this mesh's 8-window plan was refused once its windows ran, with no reason kept, and its 9-window plan at the default certificate on one mode whose relative half-width was over the default 1e-8 (FS-MODAL-HALFWIDTH-EXCEEDED); this bar was not run at the default, and under the 1e-7 row the widest certificate in its band is 2.3e-5 Hz, on the mode at 1244.515 Hz. The surface splits each grid face along its shorter diagonal, because on that cut splitting each face along the same diagonal left the boundary element's lambda/6 floor short of the band's top; on this bar the fewest panels per wavelength over the band's modes is 6.39, so all 9 modes of the band are kept.
